---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../config/site';

/*
  The severity vocabulary earns its keep one more time here. The consequences really are
  tiered, so the same four badges that classify a disaster classify what happens when
  somebody breaks the rule. Note the class is sev-warn while the id is warning.
*/
const consequences = [
  {
    sev: 'hint',
    label: 'Hint',
    what: 'I edit or delete the comment',
    detail:
      'Usually a first offence, usually somebody having a bad day. You get a note explaining what I removed and why.'
  },
  {
    sev: 'info',
    label: 'Info',
    what: 'I ask you to stop',
    detail:
      'A direct message, from me, not a bot. Nothing happens to your account. Most of these end here.'
  },
  {
    sev: 'warn',
    label: 'Warning',
    what: 'You lose commenting for a while',
    detail:
      'Seven days, then thirty. You can still read everything and your stories stay up.'
  },
  {
    sev: 'error',
    label: 'Error',
    what: 'You are done here',
    detail:
      'Account closed, comments removed. For harassment, threats, or doxxing, this is the first step and not the last one.'
  }
];

const lastChanged = new Date(SITE.conductUpdated).toLocaleDateString('en-GB', {
  day: 'numeric',
  month: 'short',
  year: 'numeric'
});
---

<Base
  title="Code of conduct"
  description="One rule: somebody just told you about the worst day of their career. Respond like it cost them something."
>
  <main id="main">
    <div class="shell read">
      <aside class="rail">
        <dl>
          <div><dt>Applies to</dt><dd>Comments, dev disasters, profiles</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Enforced by</dt><dd><a href="/about/">Michael Jolley</a></dd></div>
          <div><dt>Last changed</dt><dd>{lastChanged}</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </aside>

      <article data-pagefind-body data-pagefind-filter="kind:page">
        <header class="storyhead">
          <span class="path">conduct</span>
          <h1>
            Be the person you'd want
            <span class="squiggle"><span>reading your worst commit.</span></span>
          </h1>
          <p class="lede">
            This whole site runs on people admitting they broke something. That only works
            if admitting it is safe. So there is exactly one rule, and then some detail
            about what it means.
          </p>
        </header>

        <div class="prose conduct-body">
          <div class="callout">
            <span class="tag">The rule</span>
            <p>
              Somebody just told you about the worst day of their career. Respond like that
              cost them something, because it did.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h2>What that looks like in practice</h2>
          <p>
            Ask what you'd ask a teammate. "What would have caught this?" is a great
            comment. "How did you not know that?" is not, and the difference is not tone,
            it's whether you're trying to learn something or trying to win something.
          </p>
          <p>
            Add your version. The best threads here are four people going "oh, ours was
            worse" and then a fifth person quietly posting the config flag that prevents
            all of it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Assume the person had less information than you have now. Every disaster on
            this site looks obvious in hindsight. That's what makes it a disaster and not a
            mystery.
          </p>

          <h2>What gets you removed</h2>
          <p>
            Harassment, slurs, sexual content, and threats. Nothing clever to say about
            these, they're just out.
          </p>
          <p>
            Identifying somebody. Don't name the company, the coworker, the customer, or
            the intern. If you can work out who a story is about, keep it to yourself. If
            you post it, you're gone.
          </p>
          <p>
            Dunking. Telling somebody their outage was their own fault is both useless and
            usually true, which is exactly why it isn't worth saying.
          </p>
          <p>
            Selling things. Your product may genuinely have prevented that outage. Say so
            once, disclose that it's yours, and move on. Do it in five threads and it's
            spam.
          </p>
          <p>
            Feeding threads to a language model and pasting the output. I can tell,
            everybody can tell, and it wastes the one thing this place has, which is that a
            real person was there.
          </p>

          <h2>What happens when somebody breaks it</h2>
          <p>
            There's no committee. It's me. Here's the ladder, and I'll tell you which rung
            you're on rather than leaving you to guess.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="crows">
          {
            consequences.map((c) => (
              <div class="crow">
                <span class={`sev sev-${c.sev}`}>{c.label}</span>
                <div>
                  <h3>{c.what}</h3>
                  <p>{c.detail}</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </div>

        <div class="prose conduct-tail">
          <h2>If I get it wrong</h2>
          <p>
            Reply to the email. I've reversed moderation calls before and I'll do it again.
            What I won't do is relitigate a removal in public comments, because that turns
            somebody's bad week into a thread.
          </p>

          <h2>If it's about me</h2>
          <p>
            I'm the one who runs this, which makes reporting me awkward. Do it anyway.
            Email works, and if you'd rather not talk to me at all, say so in the report
            and I'll have somebody I trust read it first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="hero-actions conduct-acts">
          <a class="btn primary" href="/report/">Report a problem</a>
          <a class="btn" href="/all/">Browse articles and videos</a>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  .conduct-body {
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }
  .conduct-tail,
  .conduct-acts {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
  }
</style>
